\alttext(Three native boxes show the original start and its two projections, the first cross-estimates and new iterate, then the agreeing decoded point after the second update. The B-side first cross-estimate remains the old starting point.)

> The update and the correct decoded point

For nonzero beta, this implementation uses Elser's parameter choice

$$
f_A(x)=(1-\beta^{-1})P_A(x)+\beta^{-1}x,\qquad
f_B(x)=(1+\beta^{-1})P_B(x)-\beta^{-1}x,
$$
\alttext(f A is one minus the reciprocal of beta times projection A, plus x over beta. f B is one plus the reciprocal of beta times projection B, minus x over beta.)

$$
a=P_A(f_B(x)),\quad b=P_B(f_A(x)),\quad
D_\beta(x)=x+\beta(a-b).
$$
\alttext(The two cross-estimates are a equals projection A of f B and b equals projection B of f A. The new iterate is x plus beta times a minus b.)

At a fixed point with beta nonzero, a=b. Because genuine projectors return
a point in A and a point in B respectively, that common value belongs to
A∩B. The iterate x itself need not belong to either set. Nor is an arbitrary
projection of x necessarily the decoded common point. The corrected result
returns the actual b=P_B(f_A(x)) from the final evaluated update; a finite
budget or a small nonzero step does not establish exact membership in A.

For beta=1, f_A(x)=x and f_B(x)=2P_B(x)−x. Consequently
D_1=I+P_A(2P_B−I)−P_B=(I+R_A R_B)/2, where R_C=2P_C−I.
This is the Douglas–Rachford order with B reflected first. For other beta,
replacing b by P_B(x) loses the decoder identity. The distinction between
fixed iterates and recovered objects is explicit in [Elser's primary paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0111080).
The library checks shapes and finite values, but cannot prove that arbitrary
callbacks really are projections onto the claimed sets.

> A fixed point that exposed the old decoder

Take A={0,3}, B={0,2}, beta=1/4 and x=9/4. All nearest-point choices below
are strict, without ties. Then P_A(x)=3, P_B(x)=2, f_A(x)=0 and f_B(x)=1.
Both cross-projections are zero, so the iterate stays at 9/4 while the
solution is zero. Direct P_B(x)=2 is outside A. A compiled pre-change probe
returned that invalid point with a zero residual and converged=true; the
replacement decodes the actual common point instead.

On the same sets, alternating projection P_A(P_B(x)) gives three and stays
there: it cycles between B's two and A's three. This particular difference-
map control succeeds, but it is not a proof of global nonconvex convergence.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/difference_map_decode.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto a=[](const std::vector<double>& x){return dm::algorithms::project_finite_set(x,{{0},{3}});};
  const auto b=[](const std::vector<double>& x){return dm::algorithms::project_finite_set(x,{{0},{2}});};
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::difference_map_trace(a,b,{2.25},.25);const auto& s=t.steps.at(0);
  const auto direct=b(t.final_iterate),alternating=a(b({2.25}));
  if(t.result.solution!=std::vector<double>{0} || s.x_after!=std::vector<double>{2.25} || direct!=std::vector<double>{2} || a(t.result.solution)!=t.result.solution || b(t.result.solution)!=t.result.solution)return 1;
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(65,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=2.7;box.height=1.3;box.font_size=19;
  d.node("x",{0,0},std::format("x = {:.2f}\nA(x) = {:.0f}\nB(x) = {:.0f}",s.x_before[0],s.projection_a[0],s.projection_b[0]),box)
   .node("fb",{4,1.6},std::format("fB = {:.0f}",s.f_b[0]),box).node("fa",{4,-1.6},std::format("fA = {:.0f}",s.f_a[0]),box);
  box.fill={220,252,231};d.node("pa",{8,1.6},std::format("A(fB) = {:.0f}",s.estimate_a[0]),box).node("pb",{8,-1.6},std::format("B(fA) = {:.0f}",s.estimate_b[0]),box)
   .node("decoded",{12,0},std::format("Decoded = {:.0f}\nIterate = {:.2f}",t.result.solution[0],t.final_iterate[0]),box)
   .edge("x","fb").edge("x","fa").edge("fb","pa").edge("fa","pb").edge("pa","decoded").edge("pb","decoded")
   .text({6,3.2},"A = {0,3}; B = {0,2}; beta = 1/4",24)
   .text({6,-3.2},"Zero cross gap decodes the common point; direct B(x) would return invalid 2",21)
   .save_svg("build/difference_map_decode.svg");
  std::print("| quantity | actual value |\n| :--- | :--- |\n");
  std::print("| fixed iterate | {} |\n| A(x) | {} |\n| B(x) | {} |\n| fA | {} |\n| fB | {} |\n| A(fB) | {} |\n| B(fA) | {} |\n| corrected solution | {} |\n",t.final_iterate,s.projection_a,s.projection_b,s.f_a,s.f_b,s.estimate_a,s.estimate_b,t.result.solution);
  std::print("\nStep {:.6f}; gap {:.6f}; numerical stop {}. Direct B of the fixed iterate {}; A of that point {}.\n\n",s.step_norm,s.gap_norm,t.result.converged,direct,a(direct));
  std::print("Alternating projection gives {}; repeating it gives {}. Both projections fix the decoded point {}.\n",alternating,a(b(alternating)),t.result.solution);
}
