\caption(A small-beta numerical stop can hide a unit cross-projection gap. The program also checks zero-budget semantics, exact online history limits and stable large/tiny step norms.)
\alttext(Two native boxes contrast a step of one millionth passing the tolerance with a cross gap of one between disjoint singleton sets. Their connecting arrow divides by the absolute value of beta.)

> Core contracts, work and verification

The callable template signature, defaults and four-field result layout/formatter
are preserved. Correct decoding intentionally changes solution estimates when
beta differs from one or a positive iteration budget stops before agreement.
The driver calls A(x), B(x), A(f_B), B(f_A) in that order and returns the last
B cross-estimate without the old extra final B(x_after) call. With zero budget
it retains the separate historical fallback B(x0), zero residual and false
stop flag. Empty-dimensional vectors are valid if both callbacks preserve
the dimension. All controls and initial values are checked before callbacks;
every output is checked before indexing. Arithmetic failures, malformed or
nonfinite outputs and callback exceptions throw without a partial result.
Earlier callback side effects cannot be undone. Zero tolerance deliberately
disables the strict stop, even for a zero step.

Stable hypot norms avoid squaring overflow and underflow; other vector
arithmetic remains floating and must stay representable. The reusable affine
projector scales the normal and uses checked long-double intermediates, whose
extra precision/range depends on the platform. The finite-set projector
validates every candidate and chooses the earliest on a numerical distance
tie. Neither routine promises exact geometry or universal full-range arithmetic.

For k positive updates in dimension n, the driver makes 2k calls to each
projection and performs kn coordinate updates. Vector work is O(kn), in
addition to projection costs; ordinary driver storage is O(n). The rich trace
retains eight n-vectors per update plus metadata: O(k(n+1)) history. One
retained record costs 1+8n entries under its default one-million-entry cap.
That cap excludes initial/final/result vectors and transient buffers, is
checked online before the next iteration's calls, and needs space only for
actual records. Zero disables only the optional cap; capacity, counter and
entry-cost arithmetic checks remain. These counters exclude callback internals,
validation, norm work, copies and allocation, and are not timings.

Independent tests compare 12,000 recorded updates with the derived two-line
matrix over five slopes, eight beta values and 25 seeded starts per pair.
They check intermediate/returned estimates, stable norms, actual callback
order, move-only callable compatibility, ownership, zero-dimensional and
zero-budget behavior, exact history caps and overflow, malformed callbacks,
exceptions and control validation. One thousand seeded projector trials add
orthogonality/idempotence and exact small-integer nearest-point checks. The
original line cases, fixed-iterate decoding bug, disjoint drift, small-beta
false feasibility inference and convex divergence are explicit controls.
No external dataset is needed for these finite geometric checks.

> References

1. V. Elser (2003), “Phase retrieval by iterated projections”, Journal of the Optical Society of America A 20(1), 40–55. [Primary preprint](https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0111080), [published DOI](https://doi.org/10.1364/JOSAA.20.000040). The chosen reciprocal-beta parameters and decoding of the common cross-projection follow this method; the numerical contracts and actual-history controls are specified here.
